    {
      "id": "spread_transition_volatility_state_drift",
      "name": "Spread-Transition Volatility State Drift",
      "class_name": "",
      "description": "Estimate each stock's causal rolling spread-state score from the current spread relative to its trailing median and its recent change, then interact a normalized combination of trade imbalance, effective LOB imbalance, and order flow with a spread-normalization gate. Cross-sectionally rank the resulting state-conditioned directional pressure, suppressing names with negligible event activity or unreliable spreads.",
      "formula": "",
      "trading_idea": "Large-tick price formation differs materially between a stable one-tick spread state and a recently widened or rapidly normalizing spread state. A directional trade-flow and book-imbalance shock is more likely to persist when it occurs after spread compression, when the book has regained its normal quoting state, than during an active spread-widening transition where price changes are largely transitory liquidity shocks. This is falsified if subsequent returns conditional on signed pressure do not differ between stable, widening, and normalizing spread states; the expected inefficiency persists because short-lived quote-state transitions are difficult to classify robustly in real time and market makers adjust inventory before slower liquidity takers update their execution decisions.",
      "trading_idea_ids": [],
