    {
      "id": "idiosyncratic_queue_replenishment_deficit_drift",
      "name": "Idiosyncratic Queue Replenishment Deficit Drift",
      "class_name": "",
      "description": "The signal measures exponentially accumulated, activity-normalised trade and quote-flow pressure after removing the cross-sectional common component, then scales it by a trailing deficit in top-of-book depth and by pressure persistence. It is positive for buy pressure in names whose queues remain unusually depleted and negative for the symmetric sell-side state.",
      "formula": "",
      "trading_idea": "A persistent signed execution imbalance should have more forward price impact when displayed liquidity fails to replenish after the imbalance, because the local supply curve remains thin and the next tranche of the metaorder crosses progressively worse quotes. The child isolates name-specific pressure from the contemporaneous cross-sectional flow component and conditions it on a sustained deficit of displayed depth relative to the name's own recent capacity, rather than on low flow-state entropy. The edge persists because replenishing inventory and quoting against adverse-selection risk are costly, while the remaining execution schedule and off-book liquidity are not observable; it is falsified if directional 60-bar returns conditional on idiosyncratic pressure are not stronger when depth is depleted than when depth is normal.",
